WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:03.336
♪♪

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.937
-Good morning.

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:06.606
-Hi. Thank you.

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:10.209
-Diana Prince, cultural
anthropology and archaeology.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:12.812
-Barbara Minerva,
geology, gemology, lithology,

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.814
and part-time cryptozoologist.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.583
-Wow.
-I kept busy in college.

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.019
[ Telephone rings ]

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:22.321
Sorry. It's these heels.
You know? I just -- Stupid.

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:24.257
I don't know why
I was going to wear heels.

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:25.892
Scientists don't wear heels.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:28.361
-Sometimes we do.
-Right.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:29.996
Right.
-Have a nice day.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:34.167
-Those are cool! I like those.
Animal print. Rowr. [ Laughs ]

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.968
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:37.837
We're here with the wonderful
Kristen Wiig.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.005
That was a clip
from "Wonder Woman 1984."

00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:42.141
I mean, I am so excited
for this movie.

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.343
It just looks amazing.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.511
-I am so excited
for it to come out.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:47.680
-Did you know --
You play Cheetah.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.682
Did you know much
about the character?

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:55.355
-Um, I knew -- There's lots of
different versions of Cheetah

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:58.757
throughout the comic-book world.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:02.162
So, when I knew I was
going to be playing the part,

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:04.430
I did kind of, like,
research a little bit.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.631
Um, and I was like,

00:01:05.631 --> 00:01:08.000
"Well, I wonder which one
it's going to be."

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.536
And then, you know, I mean,
just through the script

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:14.340
and talking with Patty,
we just sort of landed on this.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.708
-Is it fun to be a villain?

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.743
-It's so fun.
-Yeah.

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:19.545
-It's so -- I never
get to do stuff like this.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:20.980
It's so fun, yeah.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.016
-Another thing that's really
exciting is you have a movie

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:26.619
you co-wrote with your writing
partner, Annie Mumolo,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.321
"Barb and Star
Go to Vista Del Mar."

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:31.891
-Yes.
-Which is just an A-plus --

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.192
A-plus title.

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.760
-Just the title?
Okay, great. Okay, good.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.429
-And this must be fun
to work with Annie again,

00:01:37.429 --> 00:01:39.598
who you've written with
over the years.

00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:44.370
-Yeah, it's -- She's, like,
my sister, my creative wife.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.139
I mean, she's just like --
She's the greatest, yeah.

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:48.474
-You are Barb or you are Star?

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:50.009
-I'm Star.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.611
What does Star sound like?

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.179
-[Nasally] Star sort of,
like, talks like this.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.815
[Normal voice]
We sound exactly alike.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.616
We're basically the same person.

00:01:57.616 --> 00:01:59.419
Um, but, yeah,
I'm really excited.

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.154
I'm not sure,
because of everything,

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:02.555
when it's coming out yet, but --

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:05.158
-You got to live in London
to make "Wonder Woman."

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.392
-Yes.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:07.894
-Was that an
enjoyable experience?

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.829
-I love London.
I would live there in a second.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:12.164
I -- I -- We were there, yeah,

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:15.101
for, like, I think
almost nine months total?

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:16.436
-Wow.

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:20.607
-Um, so, yeah, I felt like,
you know, a citizen.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:23.209
-Do you feel like you learned
stuff while you were there?

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.277
-I did. That's a great segue.

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:26.879
-Oh, wow. Okay.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:31.650
-Um, 'cause they have different
terms there and phrases.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.552
Like, you would ask
for something and --

00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:35.555
-Right. Like slang terms.
-They would have, like,

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:37.690
"Oh, put it in the trunk,"
and they're like, "What?"

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.492
And it's called a boot.
-Okay, got you.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.328
-Okay. I have some ones
that I'm going to quiz you on.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:43.696
-Oh, I'm excited.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:45.931
-My dress doesn't move,
so this is really hard.

00:02:45.931 --> 00:02:49.134
Okay. There we go.
All right, Seth.

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:50.637
What do you think
this is called?

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.571
-That is -- That is an elevator.

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:54.807
-It looks like a refrigerator.

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:58.244
Well, in England,
they call this a lift.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:01.013
-Oh, okay. That's very helpful.
-People know this one.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:02.915
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-It makes sense.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:04.150
-Okay.
-Okay.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.185
-A stroller.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:09.122
-They actually call this a pram.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.290
-Okay. That's very helpful, too.
I think I knew that.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.858
-Don't know where that
comes from.

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:14.928
'Cause you pram.
You pram down the street.

00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:16.763
Um, okay.

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:19.665
-Uh. Oh, is that --
Is it eyeliner?

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:22.034
-Mm-hmm. But they call it coal.
-Coal?

00:03:22.034 --> 00:03:25.104
-So if you were to ask your
makeup artist for some eyeliner,

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:27.273
they wouldn't know
what you were talking about.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:28.474
-Okay, got you. All right.

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:30.543
-And this one's
a little controversial.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.678
-It's an eraser?

00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:34.513
-It's a rubber.

00:03:34.513 --> 00:03:37.383
-Oh, that is controversial.
-It's called a rubber.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:39.852
-You get some real innuendo.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:42.354
-You can get in trouble. Yes.

00:03:42.354 --> 00:03:45.992
-Okay, I know it's an apple,
but what do they call it?

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:48.895
-This is actually called
a bambablam.

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:50.162
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:53.399
-I don't. No.
-Yep. Seth, I lived there.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:56.202
-Okay.
-Okay?

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:58.771
-It's a bicycle?

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.572
-No, this is a whiffle waffle.

00:04:00.572 --> 00:04:02.075
-A whiffle waffle?
-Yes.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:03.976
I'm going to take a ride
on my whiffle waffle.

00:04:03.976 --> 00:04:05.645
-I didn't --
I don't think that's true.

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:08.747
-It's true.
Um, and I also have a --

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:10.483
There's a couple phrases.
-Okay.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:12.485
-I'm not sure --
'Cause you didn't live there,

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.253
so you might not know
what they are.

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:16.088
-Sure, sure, sure.

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:19.225
-There's a phrase called, "Have
you been to Pipper Willy's?"

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:21.661
-Okay.
Where is Pipper Willy's?

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:23.295
-It's not a where.

00:04:23.295 --> 00:04:25.431
"Have you been
to Pipper Willy's?"

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:29.402
really translates to "Thank you,
but no thank you," in London.

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:31.770
-It seems like weird --
-That's how they say it.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.374
-So if I used it in a sentence,

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:38.111
if I said to you, like,
"Can I get you another drink?"

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:39.778
-"Have you been
to Pipper Willy's?"

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:41.514
-That seems bad.
-It's true.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:45.218
Um, and also your name, Seth...
-Oh.

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:46.752
-...would not be "Seth"
over there.

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:49.555
-Oh. What's "Seth" in London?

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:53.959
-It's Ray.
Everyone would call you Ray.

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:57.763
-So names have, like, a Br--
-Yours does.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:00.366
Not -- Some names do.
-So what's "Kristen" in London?

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.468
-Just Kristen.
It's just Kristen.

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:05.905
-What's Ray?
-There's no --

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.440
If you went over there
and said your name was Ray,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.342
they wouldn't know
what you were --

00:05:10.342 --> 00:05:12.144
they wouldn't know
what you were talking about.

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:15.247
-This is so helpful.
-I mean, now you can go.

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:16.782
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:18.050
Oh, and there's one final one

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.452
that you have to be really
careful you don't say.

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:20.353
-Oh, great.

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:22.221
-It's the word "fanny."

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:25.725
-Fanny. Gotcha.
Because it means --

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:28.060
-No.
-Okay. What does it mean?

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.562
-It's -- [Muttering]

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.964
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.466
-So when I hear British people
say "fanny,"

00:05:34.466 --> 00:05:36.869
that's what they've been
talking about?

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:39.371
Oh, my God! That was so --
-You can go now.

00:05:39.371 --> 00:05:41.373
-I can go now.
-Now you'll feel --

00:05:41.373 --> 00:05:43.676
Because I lived there, Seth,
so I know all this now.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:45.677
-And you learned all of that
in nine months?

00:05:45.677 --> 00:05:48.247
-Just that.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:50.316
-Oh, my God, Kristen, thank you
so much for being here.

00:05:50.316 --> 00:05:51.684
I cannot wait
for Saturday night.

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:53.085
-Thanks for having me.
I miss you.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:55.521
-Check out "SNL" this weekend
with Kristen Wiig.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.089
"Wonder Woman 1984"

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.292
is in select theaters and IMAX
December 25th

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:01.361
and is available the same day
on HBO Max.

